    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery and I'm running into a frustrating issue. Whenever I'm cutting the dovetails, the wood fibers are splintering right at the tips of the pins. It's ruining the edges and I can't get a clean finish. I've checked my layout, but the splintering is persistent. Any advice on tool sharpness or maybe how I should be managing the grain direction to prevent this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exploring the tool sharpness in your dovetail saw is a great starting point. Dull teeth can cause excessive tearing, leading to the splintering effect you're seeing. Try sharpening your saw blade, paying close attention to the teeth's angle and bevel, and using a guide or pencil mark to maintain consistent cuts. Additionally, work with the wood grain, cutting in the direction of the fibers, can also help reduce the splintering.",
      "source": "target",
