    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've already tried sharpening the chisels and the saw, but I'm still getting that tear-out. I'm certain the tools are razor sharp. Could it be related to the moisture content in the walnut, or maybe the way I'm approaching the final paring of the pins? I want to make sure the structural integrity is solid before I move on to the next piece.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A common culprit behind tear-out in hardwoods is poor control during the final paring of pins, especially when working on steep angles. To address this, make sure to keep your cuts very smooth and controlled, lightly stroking the chisel rather than applying too much force. Reducing the amount of waste material during the paring process can also help minimize tear-out. Keeping the sanding area well-prepared before final assembly will ensure structural integrity.",
      "source": "target",
